# the argument of phaedo-opposites.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# The universal claim's "of necessity" (S4, S17) is folded into the universal quantifier rather than given a modal operator.
# The sleep/waking analysis (S26-S27) and the greater/less, weaker/stronger cases (S4-S10) only illustrate, so they are left out.
atom	opp(x)	the members of x are opposites
atom	opp_2(x)	contradictory states with nothing between (alive vs not alive), where nothing becomes "more dead" by degrees
atom	genopp(x)	the members of x are generated out of one another
atom	process(x)	there is a passing or process from one member of x to the other
atom	living_from_dead	the living, whether things or persons, are generated from the dead
atom	souls_below	our souls exist in the world below
domain	lifedeath
P1	S17	∀x (opp(x) → genopp(x) ∧ process(x))
P2	S31	opp_2(lifedeath)
C1	S33	genopp(lifedeath)	from P1 P2
# S38 is Cebes' answer that the living come from the dead. The step from mutual generation to "living from dead" is relied on, so it is written as an assumption.
A1	-	genopp(lifedeath) → living_from_dead
C2	S39	living_from_dead	from C1 A1
# The inference to souls existing below is never stated; it needs the unstated link that if the living come from the dead, souls must exist in the world below.
A2	-	living_from_dead → souls_below
C	S41	souls_below	from C2 A2
